{"causes":["Charles Baudelaire's isolation from his family due to his mental health struggles led him to focus on his writing as a means of escape and self-expression."],"effects":["The publication of Les Fleurs du Mal helped establish the Parisian literary salons, providing a platform for writers and artists to share their work and ideas.","Baudelaire's exploration of the urban experience in Les Fleurs du Mal influenced the development of modernist literature and art, paving the way for movements such as Cubism and Futurism."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"charles-baudelaire","sourceName":"Charles Baudelaire","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"les-fleurs-du-mal","targetName":"Les Fleurs du Mal","context":"Baudelaire compiled the collection from his serial publications in L'Incroyable."},{"sourceSlug":"parisian-literary-salons","sourceName":"Parisian Literary Salons","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"les-fleurs-du-mal","targetName":"Les Fleurs du Mal","context":"The publication of Les Fleurs du Mal helped establish the Parisian literary salons."},{"sourceSlug":"edgar-degas","sourceName":"Edgar Degas","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"les-fleurs-du-mal","targetName":"Les Fleurs du Mal","context":"Degas' own artistic style was influenced by Baudelaire's exploration of the human condition."},{"sourceSlug":"claude-monet","sourceName":"Claude Monet","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"les-fleurs-du-mal","targetName":"Les Fleurs du Mal","context":"Monet's Impressionist style was also influenced by Baudelaire's work."},{"sourceSlug":"lincroyable","sourceName":"L'Incroyable","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"les-fleurs-du-mal","targetName":"Les Fleurs du Mal","context":"The serial publications of Les Fleurs du Mal were initially published in L'Incroyable."},{"sourceSlug":"paris","sourceName":"Paris","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"les-fleurs-du-mal","targetName":"Les Fleurs du Mal","context":"Baudelaire's work was influenced by his experiences in Paris."},{"sourceSlug":"france","sourceName":"France","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"les-fleurs-du-mal","targetName":"Les Fleurs du Mal","context":"Baudelaire's work reflects the cultural and intellectual currents of 19th-century France."},{"sourceSlug":"symbolism","sourceName":"Symbolism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"les-fleurs-du-mal","targetName":"Les Fleurs du Mal","context":"Baudelaire's exploration of the human condition influenced the development of Symbolist poetry."},{"sourceSlug":"edgar-allan-poe","sourceName":"Edgar Allan Poe","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"les-fleurs-du-mal","targetName":"Les Fleurs du Mal","context":"Poe's own exploration of the darker aspects of human nature was influenced by Baudelaire's work."}],"places":[{"name":"Paris","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"London","role":"Influence"},{"name":"Rouen","role":"Place of Execution"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Les Fleurs du mal","type":"Reference article","slug":"les-fleurs-du-mal"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q216578","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Fleurs_du_mal"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Les Fleurs du mal contributed to the historical record of the Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Les Fleurs du mal ('The Flowers of Evil') is a collection of poems by the French poet Charles Baudelaire (1821–1867), first published in Paris on 25 June 1857 by the publisher Poulet-Malassis, immediately condemned as obscene by a French court (Baudelaire, his publisher, and his printer were convicted; six poems were ordered suppressed; the conviction was overturned only in 1949), and recognised in the 20th century as the most important collection of French poetry of the 19th century and a founding document of literary Modernism. The first edition contained 100 poems; the second edition (1861, after the suppression of six poems) contained 126 poems; a posthumous third edition (1868) added 25 more, for a total of 151.

Les Fleurs du mal is organised into six sections: 'Spleen et Idéal' (the largest, containing the great lyric poems of beauty, melancholy, and the tension between spiritual aspiration and sensual experience), 'Tableaux Parisiens' (urban poems of Paris, the anonymous modern city), 'Le Vin', 'Fleurs du Mal', 'Révolte', and 'La Mort' (death as the final escape). The collection's central subject is the tension between spleen (ennui, acedia, spiritual deadness) and idéal (spiritual aspiration, beauty, transcendence) — a tension that Baudelaire finds in the most repulsive subjects (decay, prostitution, the city's underworld, death) as well as the most beautiful. The famous opening poem, 'Au Lecteur' ('To the Reader'), addresses the reader directly as a fellow sinner: 'Hypocrite lecteur, — mon semblable, — mon frère!' ('Hypocrite reader — my likeness — my brother!')

Baudelaire's innovations — the concept of 'correspondances' (synesthetic connections between senses, the idea that perfumes, colours, and sounds correspond to one another and to spiritual states), the idea of the poet as an alienated figure ('spleen'), the urban flaneur, the erotic poem as a form of spiritual investigation — inaugurated the Symbolist movement (Verlaine, Rimbaud, Mallarmé) and, through Symbolism, Modernism. T. S. Eliot directly quoted 'Au Lecteur' in The Waste Land (1922).","subjects":["French Literature","Modern Era","Charles Baudelaire","Poetry","Symbolism","Modernism","19th Century","French Poetry"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Les Fleurs du Mal had a profound impact on modern French literature and culture, shaping the development of Symbolism and influencing artists such as Edgar Degas and Claude Monet.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
